    "turn_text": "And I would say, well, you know, look, I always like to reach across the aisle. But sometimes I had to reach inside the aisle. I had to reach inside the Democratic caucus to try to pull people together, too, to get some things done. And that's, I think, has been a problem that we've seen in the last few months as well. Yeah, but I look at this infrastructure bill and I have friends who were in Congress over the years and they've always said, you know, in past decades, infrastructure was kind of a yawn vote. Everybody wanted it. Everybody wanted to bring projects home. Why was this so difficult to get? And why is it so difficult for us to get, you know, the investments that we need in the future done? I mean, for me, it's about I want to get things done, but I'm in this weird spot where we're fighting over things we used to really agree on. Yeah, you're right. But now let's go back a little bit and look at some of the history. First of all, we've had for the last like three presidents wanted to get giant infrastructure bills passed. It couldn't really get done because people kept loading the bill up with things that didn't involve infrastructure. So what happened this year is that you had the members of the Senate, you had 10 or 12 members of the US Senate, equal number of Republicans and Democrats said, we want an infrastructure bill. And they worked very hard. And they stripped down to the basics of real, true infrastructure, the kind of infrastructure that you're talking about that in the past everybody tended to agree on. And they were able to get that done. We're talking about roads. We're talking about bridges. We're talking about broadband. We're talking about hard infrastructure that is crumbling around the United States these days. But then it shifted over to the House of Representatives. And certain members of the Democratic Caucus weren't satisfied with just the hard infrastructure. They wanted what they called the human infrastructure, the Build Back Better plan, and they wanted it to go together. So I really believe Rick had the infrastructure bill that passed the Senate with 19 Republican votes. Remember that? Very important. 19 Republican votes in the Senate. If the House had taken that vote early on, you would have had a lot of Republicans who voted for it. You wouldn't have been overwhelming. It wouldn't have been a majority. But you'd have had more than 12, I think, which is all the ones out of there. But for whatever reason, folks in the Democratic Party started fighting among themselves. They held that vote. And then once they did, it became a political tool. You saw the party fighting among itself. You saw differences between that, the Build Back Better plan. And every time that happens, it gave the other side the political ammunition that they needed to say, we're going to vote against this. We're going to deny they weren't at that table in those fights. And it was a combination of things. But at the end of the day, I was at the ceremony where the bill was signed by the president the other day it was an incredible day it was a glorious day there were republicans there there were people from",
