    "turn_text": "forward, I think, find that common ground, the things that we agree on, that people should be able to make a reasonable living. People should have health insurance, have retirement security, have dignity in the work that they do. But sadly, I'm looking around and the ability for workers to join and form unions have been almost eviscerated. I look at what's going on on Amazon. We just now find out that because of all of the illegal activity that Amazon did in your state in Bessemer, they're now going to get to run another election. And I don't see that as a real penalty. There should have been a union imposed. They should have been forced to bargain. They should have been forced to do the right thing. But yet now they got a, well, another bite at the apple to beat their employees up. I do want to get your thoughts on what's going on there. Well, you know, first of all, I'm really happy to see that Amazon is going to have to have another vote. I mean, that's the wall. That's the only thing that can do. They can't force employees to unionize in a right to work state like Alabama, but they give them another body at the Apple. And I think that by showing really the links that Amazon went to to deny this vote. I'm hoping that it will help these folks organize and you get a little bit better job and get more people out there to vote. I was out there with Reverend Barber and some others in support of those folks. But you know, Rick, I think that it goes beyond right now. Let's just be really honest about this. With organized labor, especially in the South, it goes beyond just the right for a living wage and for good benefits and things like that. There are a lot of cultural issues that organized labor and the members have that I don't think that has always been addressed. And we got to make sure that people know what those are and the fact that we can understand all of those issues. But I am hopeful that with what we're seeing in the bill back better plan, what we're seeing in the infrastructure bill, I mean the infrastructure bill alone is going to do more for organized labor than any piece of legislation probably in the last 30 or 40 years. It's really amazing. I just hope that we can get not the leadership and organize later, but the rank and file. The guys out on the line there in Pennsylvania are here in Alabama, the unions that we have. I hope we get them to understand what's been done for them to help them and their families. And I think it's really important",
